& [CoreConclusion] is take Cantor to a semantic execution model before making it a model-internal llama.cpp component
  + Cantor already models language as inspectable paths through semantic identities, operations, relationships, faculty transforms, constraints, uncertainty, and proof
  + a learned Cantor becomes a literal statistical semantic language model when it estimates candidate semantic states or transitions conditioned on prompt, purpose, fabric, and prior state
  + an ordinary LLM remains the broad interpreter and articulator while Cantor remains the precise semantic path model and authority seam
  + the first joined system should use an external duplex controller, ordinary llama.cpp tool calls, private provider contexts, and one versioned Cantor JunctionSession
  + one user-visible shared-inference job may contain many model forward or decode passes without being one literal shared transformer forward pass
  = concise: build a joined semantic reasoning loop now; defer hidden-state joining until the external loop exposes a measured limitation

& [CantorLanguageModelIdentity] distinguishes semantic modeling from surface generation
  + [semantic_vocabulary] is admitted identities, operations, roles, relationships, reference forms, constraints, faculty programs, faults, and proofs
  + [semantic_state] is a ResolutionLattice or admitted semantic snapshot under one purpose and fabric digest
  + [semantic_transition] is a proposed, rejected, remasked, qualified, or admitted state delta
  + [conditioning] is prompt stimulus, purpose, signed anchor bundle, participant role, current state, unresolved set, and prior path
  + [path_score] may express deterministic support, calibrated probability, cost, risk, or expected information gain without collapsing those meanings
  + [surface_projection] is natural-language prompt, tool packet, explanation, or response rendered from semantic state
  = must: call Cantor a learned language model only when a trained artifact estimates semantic candidates or transitions
  = may: call the current deterministic form a semantic transition model, semantic execution model, or language-model coprocessor
  - never: imply that proof-carrying deterministic meaning is stored only in learned weights

& [SemanticCoherencePath] formalizes the user's central intuition
  * receive a prompt stimulus and bind its immutable digest
  * resolve the active purpose, authority, fabric, participants, and completion contract
  * form a bounded ResolutionLattice containing candidate identities, operations, relationships, and uncertainty
  * activate materially pertinent faculty programs and exact SOP queries
  * let one or more providers propose semantic transitions from their private contexts
  * validate proposals against identity, evidence, boundary, authority, constraint, and proof gates
  * admit, qualify, challenge, remask, branch, or reject each transition
  * project the admitted state and unresolved frontier into the next provider checkpoint
  * stop on completion evidence, unresolved block, fault, no gain, or budget
  = conclusion: the prompt stimulates a governed semantic path rather than directly programming inaccessible neural attention weights

& [ForwardPassDelineation] prevents three mechanisms from being confused
  + [model_forward_or_decode_pass] computes model outputs from the provider-visible context and private model state
  + [checkpointed_shared_inference_job] coordinates several private model calls, tool calls, Cantor transitions, and continuations under one JunctionSession
  + [literal_joint_forward_pass] lets distinct models exchange or jointly mutate activations, caches, attention, or cross-model latent state before a forward computation completes
  + [selected_first_meaning] is checkpointed_shared_inference_job presented as one virtual semantic pipeline
  + [deferred_research_meaning] is literal_joint_forward_pass
  = must: state which meaning every experiment implements
  - never: describe an external tool result appended to a later request as in-place mutation of an already-running forward pass

& [CurrentLlamaCPPBoundary] is verified against official master documentation on 2026-08-05
  + [supported] is OpenAI-compatible chat completion, function calling, parallel decoding slots, continuous batching, context checkpoints, prompt-cache save and restore, and provider-private inference
  + [supported_but_internal] is llama-server GET and POST tools endpoints intended for its Web UI and subject to change
  + [maintainer_boundary] is complex external API and server-side agentic loops should remain outside llama-server backend code
  + [maintainer_boundary] is intermediate activation exposure unsupported as a stable server API and eval callbacks unsuitable as a general multi-sequence API
  + [RPC_boundary] llama.cpp RPC distributes one model's tensor and KV computation over devices and is not semantic negotiation between independent inference participants
  + [speculative_boundary] draft-target token verification accelerates decoding and is not by itself cooperative semantic deliberation
  + [official_function_calling] is https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/docs/function-calling.md
  + [official_server] is https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/server/README.md
  + [official_server_design] is https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/server/README-dev.md
  + [official_RPC] is https://github.com/ggml-org/llama.cpp/blob/master/tools/rpc/README.md
  = conclusion: standard function calling plus an external controller is the native first seam; llama.cpp core modification is unnecessary for the first joined proof

& [ExistingCantorFit] is substantial
  + the prior unmodified llama.cpp probe already emitted a structured tool call and imported the deterministic result through a subsequent inference request
  + the CLI and read-only Codex MCP adapter are implemented, proven, and semantically equivalent within their current query scope
  + the Cooperative Inference Junction already defines participant identity, shared purpose, anchor digest, exchange packets, barriers, versions, reconciliation, and faults
  + the Notification Profile already defines buffered, invalidating, and stop-required state changes plus stale-candidate handling
  + the Faculty Deliberation Runtime already treats model and tool calls as explicit checkpoints and supports isolated or independent provider jobs
  + the Attention Engineering profile already selects explicit external state before literal hidden-state intervention
  + no current crate implements JunctionSession, SemanticExchangePacket, stateful cantor.exchange, inference notification, or the shared-inference scheduler
  = conclusion: the new idea is an integration and learned-semantic-model delta over formed architecture, not a new subsystem identity

& [CantorDuplexSemanticPipeline] is the selected virtual execution shape
  + [controller] owns provider scheduling, cancellation policy, retries, timeouts, and transport
  + [Cantor] owns one JunctionSession, purpose contract, signed anchors, participant identities, semantic state, version, barriers, gates, reconciliation, and archive
  + [participant_A] is Codex, a llama.cpp slot, or another provider with one private context
  + [participant_B] is a separate llama.cpp slot or provider with one private context
  + [tool_contract] is provider-neutral cantor.exchange with propose, challenge, qualify, cite, reconcile, poll, acknowledge, yield, and stop operations
  + [projection] is the smallest admitted semantic frame and unresolved set required by each participant
  + [continuation] appends the attributed Cantor tool result to that participant's later provider request
  + [concurrency] allows participants to reason independently between semantic barriers
  + [commit] occurs only after current-version Cantor validation and Observer disposition
  = must: preserve independent authorship, private contexts, and stale-state rejection

& [VirtualPipelineCycle] is multi-pass and may appear continuous to the user
  * open one JunctionSession and freeze purpose, anchors, participants, limits, and completion evidence
  * project version zero into each participant context
  * run participants concurrently or in a declared order until one emits cantor.exchange or reaches a barrier
  * validate and append the attributed packet under compare-and-append state versioning
  * notify other participant controllers of the new state version
  * buffer ordinary updates or invalidate state-dependent in-flight candidates according to urgency
  * project admitted peer packets, directives, and residuals into later provider requests
  * repeat challenge, support, revision, and reconciliation rounds under bounded no-gain and budget rules
  * emit one proof-carrying result plus residual disagreement and complete replay lineage

& [LlamaCPPIntegrationProgression] answers how far to take the coupling
  + [stage_0_current] use existing Cantor CLI or MCP query and the passed unmodified llama.cpp tool-call seam
  + [stage_1_next] implement the canonical deterministic two-mock-participant JunctionSession proof with no model provider
  + [stage_2] replay the same fixture through two private llama.cpp server contexts using standard chat-completion tool calls
  + [stage_3] connect one Codex participant and one llama.cpp participant to the same provider-neutral Cantor junction after a stateful MCP exchange surface is separately authorized
  + [stage_4] add semantic diffusion or a learned transition-order policy in shadow mode over the same recorded junction traces
  + [stage_5] consider a custom llama.cpp sampler, grammar, semantic stop marker, or decode hook only for a signed unmet latency, intervention, or constraint requirement
  + [stage_6] treat activation, cache, cross-attention, or literal joint-forward-pass coupling as a separate provider-specific research program requiring training and off-target evaluation
  = must: retain the same semantic packet and proof protocol through every stage
  = must: stop at the first stage satisfying the measured requirement

& [ModificationTrigger] is evidence rather than attraction to proximity
  + [latency_trigger] checkpoint serialization and reprompt cost prevents the signed response-time requirement
  + [control_trigger] required semantic constraint cannot be expressed through prompt, grammar, tool, or post-generation validation
  + [information_trigger] an essential model signal exists only inside an activation or cache and cannot be replaced by an inspectable projection
  + [training_trigger] reviewed traces show a stable learned task whose gain cannot be achieved by routing or external adaptation
  + [replay_trigger] provider-level callbacks can preserve deterministic replay, versioning, and rollback at acceptable cost
  + [negative_gate] no llama.cpp fork when ordinary tool continuation and external orchestration satisfy the requirement

& [CustomLlamaCPPResearchOptions] are deliberately separate
  + [semantic_sampler] masks or reranks candidate tokens from a Cantor-projected grammar or constraint state
  + [semantic_stop_and_resume] yields at declared semantic delimiters, invokes external Cantor, appends a result, and resumes through a later decode checkpoint
  + [continuous_conditioning] maps Cantor state to learned prefix, adapter, or cross-attention input and requires compatible model training
  + [activation_exchange] exposes selected intermediate representations between models and requires a new security, stability, and semantic-preservation contract
  + [joint_architecture] trains a model whose computation natively includes a Cantor semantic module
  - never: merge arbitrary KV caches or hidden tensors from independently diverged contexts and call the result coherent

& [FirstExperiment] is the canonical deterministic junction proof, not a llama.cpp fork
  + [participants] is two deterministic mock participants with distinct identities and private state
  + [purpose] is one bounded ambiguous semantic decision over one test-signed SOP anchor bundle
  + [exchange] is proposal, evidence-grounded challenge, revision, reconciliation, and one preserved residual disagreement
  + [versions] is exact compare-and-append with one intentionally stale write rejected
  + [authority_fault] is one unsigned peer claim rejected as authority laundering
  + [notification] is one state change delivered, one duplicate ignored, and one in-flight stale candidate prevented from commitment
  + [replay] is byte-equivalent normalized result and trace under repeated execution
  + [provider_replay] substitutes two llama.cpp contexts only after deterministic proof closure
  = conclusion: this fixture directly tests the pipeline semantics before provider behavior can obscure protocol faults

& [EvaluationSet] measures whether joining models helps
  + [semantic_gain] is newly supported identities, relationships, constraints, contradictions, or corrections per admitted round
  + [independent_contribution] is participant-specific material not copied from a peer packet
  + [false_consensus] is agreement without independent evidence
  + [correction_quality] is wrong candidate identification and successful qualified revision
  + [proof_completeness] is source, version, participant, gate, and disposition coverage
  + [stale_commit_rate] must be zero
  + [authority_bypass] must be zero
  + [termination] is completion, residual disagreement, no gain, fault, or budget without deadlock
  + [work] is provider calls, tokens, injected bytes, latency, memory, tool calls, and Cantor transitions
  + [baseline] is one model with the same total token and tool budget plus ordinary Cantor query access

& [CandidateRequirementSet] is CSLP-001 through CSLP-024
  + [CSLP-001] define Cantor as an inspectable semantic execution model independently of whether a learned model exists
  + [CSLP-002] reserve literal learned-language-model identity for an artifact that estimates semantic states or transitions
  + [CSLP-003] bind every semantic path to prompt, purpose, fabric, participant, prior state, and proof lineage
  + [CSLP-004] distinguish one provider decode pass, one checkpointed shared-inference job, and one literal joint forward pass
  + [CSLP-005] select an external duplex controller and ordinary llama.cpp tool calls as the first provider seam
  + [CSLP-006] preserve private provider contexts and make Cantor the sole shared semantic state authority
  + [CSLP-007] implement shared inference through versioned attributed packets and semantic barriers rather than token-stream or hidden-state crossfeeding
  + [CSLP-008] preserve provider-neutral cantor.exchange operations, schemas, faults, and proof
  + [CSLP-009] implement and prove two deterministic mock participants before two llama.cpp contexts
  + [CSLP-010] replay the same fixture through two llama.cpp contexts without changing semantic expectations
  + [CSLP-011] require separate authority before exposing a stateful Codex or MCP junction tool
  + [CSLP-012] buffer, invalidate, stop, replay, or reject provider work according to versioned notification urgency
  + [CSLP-013] prevent stale candidates and peer claims from acquiring semantic or effect authority
  + [CSLP-014] keep complex agent-loop policy outside llama-server in the initial architecture
  + [CSLP-015] distinguish llama.cpp parallel slots, unified KV allocation, RPC offload, speculative decoding, and cooperative semantic inference
  + [CSLP-016] measure against one-model equal-budget and ordinary-query baselines
  + [CSLP-017] record semantic gain, independent contribution, consensus faults, correction, proof, staleness, termination, and work
  + [CSLP-018] require zero authority bypass and stale commitment in every stage
  + [CSLP-019] admit semantic diffusion or learned transition policy only in shadow mode after deterministic junction traces exist
  + [CSLP-020] require a signed measured trigger before custom sampler, decode hook, activation, cache, or architecture modification
  + [CSLP-021] preserve semantic packets and proof across provider and intervention replacements
  + [CSLP-022] make literal intra-forward-pass or latent exchange a separate provider-specific research track
  + [CSLP-023] forbid arbitrary merging of independently diverged KV caches or hidden activations
  + [CSLP-024] stop integration progression at the least invasive stage that satisfies the signed requirement

& [Decision] is reuse formed architecture and add a bounded integration progression
  + [canonical_authority] existing junction, notification, faculty, intervention, query, CLI, and MCP specifications remain authoritative within their signed scopes
  + [new_canonical_authority] is absent
  + [new_implementation_authority] is absent
  + [llama_cpp_modification_authority] is absent
  + [schedule_effect] add CSLP-SJS-01 to reconcile the semantic-model delta with existing junction authority and then perform a readiness review for the deterministic two-mock junction proof
  + [first_next_step] is determine whether current completed single-job, supervised mock-loop, resident-service, CLI, MCP, and live Codex proofs satisfy every explicit prerequisite of MinimumJunctionProof
  + [reasoning_depth] current reconciliation requires deeper architecture reasoning; the later closed-schema two-mock fixture becomes Spark-suitable after signed activation authority exists
